    <Program class="ca.nrcan.gc.OEE.ERS.ErsProgram">
        <Labels>
            <English>EnerGuide Rating System</English>
            <French>Système de cote ÉnerGuide</French>
        </Labels>
        <Version major="15" minor="5" build="8">
            <Labels>
                <English>v15.5b8</English>
                <French>v15.5b8</French>
            </Labels>
        </Version>
        <SdkVersion major="1" minor="16">
            <Labels>
                <English>v1.16</English>
                <French>v1.16</French>
            </Labels>
        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;

There is some excessive air leakage in your home that appears to be associated with a few key areas: the chimney for the current furnace, the 5" open combustion air pipe for the furnace and around most of the older wood windows and the wood entry door.



Upgrading the furnace will eliminate the current chimney and the open combustion air pipe. Upgrading the wood windows and the one wood door to new ENERGY STAR® qualified units (full frame replacement - not just inserts) will significantly reduce that air leakage to the point where you will no longer need to install a sheet of plastic over them during the fall, winter &amp; early spring. Overall, you should be able to achieve a 10% - 15% reduction in air leakage (heat loss) from your home. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a 12.5% reduction in air leakage, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 154&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;5.1 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <windows>

I have modeled an upgrade to all of the existing windows in your home to ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the existing windows with ENERGY STAR® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 237 &lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;7.8 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <doors>

I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading the older wood entrance door to an ENERGY STAR® qualified polyurethane insulation filled door rated for climate zone 3.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the older wood door with an ENERGY STAR® qualified unit rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 80&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.6 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</doors>
                <VentilationSystem />
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house has a 50 USG electric hot water tank, that has an estimated energy factor of 0.82, and the cost of electricity is very high in Fort Simpson. If you switch your furnace to propane, I would recommend you consider replacing the electric hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,547&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil furnace is labeled as 82.1% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE - the average efficiency over the heating season). It vents up a 5" chimney, and there is also a 5" open combustion air pipe.



I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This furnace will draw its' combustion air directly from outside, and vent combustion out through a sidewall pipe. This will eliminate the current chimney &amp; it will eliminate the 5" open combustion air pipe.



&lt;b&gt;NOTE 1:&lt;/b&gt; The heat supply and return air design for the current furnace is set up so that the furnace is immediately drawing the heated air right back inside the furnace. Based on the way the fins on the supply &amp; return grates are positioned, the heat supply near the ceiling is directing the air downward and the return air near the floor is drawing down air from above. If you stand about 8' in in front of the furnace supply/return ducts while the furnace is operating you will feel the warm air flowing towards your face &amp; neck ......... exactly where the return air is drawing air back in from. You should talk with a heating contractor to have the supply &amp; return ducts re-designed to achieve better warm air circulation throughout the home. Perhaps the supply duct could be run along the ceiling to the rear of the house, and install a heat supply duct through the wall into each bedroom?



&lt;b&gt;NOTE 2:&lt;/b&gt; Please note that your wood stove is currently drawing its' combustion air from inside the house, however you will see (near the bottom at the rear of the wood stove) there is a combustion air inlet where a dedicated combustion air pipe can be connected, to draw in combustion air from the outside of the house. If you eliminate the current combustion air pipe for the current furnace, your heating contractor will want to install another one that is sealed directly to the back of the woodstove.





&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your heating system with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,265&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;27.9 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------









</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home currently has no ventilation:



1. The kitchen range hood is an interior recirculation model.

2. There is no bathroom fan

3. There was no clothes dryer installed at the time of my visit.



Overall, your house currently has enough natural air leakage (furnace chimney &amp; combustion air supply, the wood stove chimney, around/through windows &amp; doors) that excess humidity should not be an issue. However, as you complete some of the upgrades outlined in the report, your house will become more air tight. You may want to consider installing a bathroom exhaust fan, vented to the outside, that can be operated whenever there is excess humidity in the house or when you would like to exhaust stale or cooking smells from the house.



As outlined elsewhere within this report, the woodstove should have a dedicated outside combustion air supply directly connected to the pipe at the back of the wood stove.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



There are currently no exhaust fans in your house (range hood fan, bathroom fan or dryer) that would compete with the chimney's of the furnace or wood stove. To help ensure this in the future, this report recommends a new direct vent, high efficiency furnace and also connecting a dedicated outside combustion air pipe to the back of your woodstove.



During my assessment of your home, there were smoke detectors in the house, however I did not see any carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Fort Simpson&lt;/b&gt;



The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Fort Simpson:

Electricity - $18.90 min, $0.31 per kWh up to the TPSP threshold, $0.70 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $1.07 per litre 

Propane - $0.60 per litre

Wood - $367.50 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $7.35 per bag, $393.75 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Disclaimer&lt;/b&gt;



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Your Energy Advisor&lt;/b&gt;



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



&lt;b&gt;Ken Baigent

Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance&lt;/b&gt;

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



&lt;b&gt;Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!&lt;/b&gt;

</AdditionalComments>
            </RURComments>
